on\n- Comments\n- Signature of the commissioning agent\n- Signature of the dummy box\n\nThis is what the commissioning agent needs to complete after the project is finished.\n\n说话人2: 01:46:52 - 01:47:23\n\nSource: {'char_count': 1016, 'pk': 462470511333799580, 'rerank_score': 0.0001852257432653108}\nContent: 说话人2: 01:01:39 - 01:02:04\n\nOr the certificate, this is the letter, in this format. I’ve omitted all the other details, so it’s written like this, and it needs to be personally stamped. This is the certificate, or you could call it a letter.\n\nAnother one is this, which is essentially the fire alarm system. Every time, according to the local regulations and standards, as long as you make any changes to the reference system—whether it’s a reduction, addition, modification, or even just relocating it—you need to perform a verification again. Verification means confirming that the system is still functional. And who is responsible for doing this?\n\n说话人2: 01:02:04 - 01:02:45\n\nActually, it’s a third party that conducts this verification, which follows a special format. The company is called Tomy Fire Alarm Verification. After completing the process, there will be a summary showing yes or no—whether it passed or not. Here you’ll also find the name and signature of the technician who performed the verification.\n\nSource: {'char_count': 999, 'pk': 462470511333799657, 'rerank_score': 0.00011323437855834611}\nContent: Bid documents are usually divided into two parts: technical and financial. The financial part is essentially the pricing, including the total cost and the hourly rates of personnel. This helps the client evaluate additional work if needed. The technical section covers your company’s background, team experience, past projects, project management approach, understanding of the project, plans, and proposed workflows. If the project is awarded to you, these details form the technical proposal, which can range from just 8–10 pages.\n\n说话人2: 02:15:17 - 02:17:41\n\nThe document can be up to a hundred pages long. In the technical proposal, you are required to provide reference projects, meaning similar projects you’ve worked on before. You need to list detailed information about these projects, including project management details. Essentially, this means providing contact information for the clients you’ve previously collaborated with, so they can verify your claims. This is a mandatory section.", name='hybrid_RAG_retrieve', id='bb811d8d-b7b3-437e-a12d-7ff5b2f143ab', tool_call_id='cc748002-9237-44ca-ac38-472f0c601d62')]

